                <pb facs="0158" n="54" sameAs="frus:frus1932v03/pg54"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/3613: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-01-23T13:00:00+08:00">January 23, 1932—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 23—2:40 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>113. Following from American Consul General at Harbin: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“January 21, 5 p.m. Commissioner of Foreign Affairs informed Chamberlain
                            today that Pinhsien last bombed on 16th and Yuanshu, Wuchang and
                            neighboring places last few days, Japanese aeroplane flew over Harbin
                            yesterday, that Cheng Jun, head of the Pinhsien Government, is
                            determined to hold out against the new regime until the receipt of
                            orders from Nanking and mat local newspapers are so fearful of Japanese
                            influence that they dare not publish reports of bombing.</p>
                        <list>
                            <label>2.</label>
                            <item>I have no confirmation of reports of bombing. Japanese have
                                informed me that it is sure that agreement has been reached whereby
                                Pinhsien Government will be dissolved. I believe that it cannot
                                maintain itself.</item>
                            <label>3.</label>
                            <item>Ohashi left Harbin by airplane for Mukden yesterday evidently to
                                consult with the Japanese there and later at Kirin.</item>
                            <label>4.</label>
                            <item>It is reported that Doihara left Tokyo for Harbin yesterday. His
                                expected arrival here is looked upon with apprehension by the
                                Chinese authorities.”</item>
                        </list></quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
